From: Aurélien Pommel <aurelien.pommel@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panfrost: hold a PM reference across the job timeout

A job that times out and then faults while the timeout handler runs takes
the system down. The fault is handled by the job IRQ, which signals the
job's fence and drops its runtime PM reference. With no other job queued
the GPU autosuspends 50 ms later, and on RK3568 that disables its clocks
and asserts its reset. The timeout handler then writes JOB_INT_MASK at the
start of panfrost_reset() and the access aborts as an asynchronous SError.

The core dump makes the window wide: it copies every BO of the job, which
took about a second for Dolphin, while the GPU kept running and faulted
again.

Hold a reference from the register reads through the reset. If the GPU
already suspended, resuming it soft-resets it, which the reset needs anyway.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.c
@@ -787,6 +787,15 @@
 		return DRM_GPU_SCHED_STAT_NO_HANG;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The job drops its PM reference if it faults from here on, and the
+	 * core dump can outlast the autosuspend delay: hold one for the reset.
+	 */
+	if (pm_runtime_resume_and_get(pfdev->base.dev)) {
+		dev_err(pfdev->base.dev, "gpu sched timeout, cannot resume the GPU\n");
+		return DRM_GPU_SCHED_STAT_NO_HANG;
+	}
+
 	dev_err(pfdev->base.dev, "gpu sched timeout, js=%d, config=0x%x, status=0x%x, head=0x%x, tail=0x%x, sched_job=%p",
 		js,
 		job_read(pfdev, JS_CONFIG(js)),
@@ -800,6 +809,8 @@
 	atomic_set(&pfdev->reset.pending, 1);
 	panfrost_reset(pfdev, sched_job);
 
+	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(pfdev->base.dev);
+
 	return DRM_GPU_SCHED_STAT_RESET;
 }
 
